 	SetTerm -- By Brian C. White			-- Copyright (c) 1991 by Dynamic Software SystemsSetTerm is a program to allow easy changing of the "TERM" environmentvariable.  It also copies the chosen termcap entry into the "TERMCAP"environment variable so programs can access it much faster.  This issupported by the Microware "termlib" library, so all programs linkedwith that will make use of this feature.This archive contains:	copying			- Rules on copying (GNU General Public License)	setterm.doc		- this file	setterm			- 68000 executable code	setterm.c		- C source code for SetTerm	sys.setterm		- "/dd/sys/setterm" information file for setterm	termcap.extra	- Additional termcap entriesThis is version 2.0 of SetTerm.  Documentation follows and is within the "sys.setterm" file.Please let me know if you have any comments, suggestions, gripes, etc.										Brian C. White------------------------------------------------------------------------------| OOOO   OOOO  OOOO |           Dynamic Software Systems                     || O   O O     O     |--------------------------------------------------------|| O   O  OOO   OOO  | P.O.  Box 1565  | Delphi:    BRIANWHITE                || O   O     O     O | Esterhazy, Sask | CIS:       70313,3344                || OOOO  OOOO  OOOO  | S0A 0X0  Canada | Internet:  70313.3344@compuserve.com |------------------------------------------------------------------------------Support for sh and ksh was added to version 2.0 by Martin Gregorie, who alsoadded a section to this document describing the extensions. He added the oversized command traps as well.	Gregorie@logica.comInstallation============Copy "setterm" to your execution directory and "sys.setterm" to "/dd/sys/setterm" and edit the defaults to suit your installation.How the program works===================== When SetTerm runs, it starts by getting the name of the login device. Ifpossible this is extracted from the PORT environment variable, but if thisis unset it gets the device name from the program's input path (normally path 1, but path 0 if the "-i" option is set).It then sets up a default terminal type.  If a terminal type is supplied as a parameter this is used as the default. Otherwise, it checks the "TERM" environment variable.  If defined, that will be used for the default type.  Should the "TERM" variable not be defined, as is the case with a new login, SetTerm will read the file "/dd/sys/setterm" to try and match the current port with a default type specified in that file. (See the sample file "sys.setterm" that came with this file for more information.)  As a last resort, should no match be found, the terminal type will default to "dumb".If a valid terminal type was supplied as the command line parameter or if the default in "/ss/sys/setterm" is marked as "known for sure" the program will complete its task silently. In all other situations the default is shown and the user asked to confirm the choice. A new type can be specified, or the default can be accepted by pressing <ENTER>.  At this point, the termcap file is searched for a match.  If no match is found, the user is prompted again. This is repeated until the terminal type matches an entry in the termcap file. During this input is read from the path selected by the "-i" option. The default suits the Microware shell but the "-i" option shouldbe present for the "sh" and "ksh" shells.Setterm now reads the SHELL environment variable to determine which type of shell it is dealing with. The standard tsmon sets SHELL from the commandfield in "/dd/sys/passwords"; if you are not using tsmon you must make sure that SHELL is set appropriately before running setterm. If SHELL is not defined the standard Microware shell name "shell" is assumed. Setterm now generates commands to set the PORT, TERM, LINES, COLUMNS and TERMCAP environment variables. The syntax of the commands is selected by the shell name and is hard wired into setterm. The output file depends on the "-s" option. If it is absent output goes to /pipe/setterm. Supplying the optioncauses output to be written to the standard output. See below for the reason behind this.If you want to suppress creation of the TERMCAP environment variable, usethe -x option. This is needed if your shell can't handle the size of thecommand generated for a complex terminal definition.Note:  When the value of the TERMCAP environment variable is being set up all occurences of quotes, whether single ('), double (") or reverse (`), are converted to their octal equivalent (\ooo) so as not to interfere with the shell.OS/9 provides no direct way for a program to pass changes in environment variables back to the calling shell, which is why commands to apply the required changes are written to a file for processing by the shell after setterm has completed. Output uses proper critical-section locking for correct output to the named pipe used by default. The commands and and the best place to write the file are shell-specific:Standard Microware shell------------------------	The "-si" options are never used with this shell so that input is read 	from path 1 (never redirected by this shell) and output is written to	"/pipe/setterm". The output contains a "setenv" command for each 	environment variable being set. Each command appears on a separate line.	Setterm will be usually be run from the .login script and the 	"profile" command used to execute the commands in "/pipe/setterm",	although another script could be be called via "profile" to do the 	same thing.  For example, a simple script might be:		setterm		profile /pipe/setterm	This script would be called by "profile <script>" or included as part of	".login".  Unfortunately, the shell won't search the "PATH" for shell 	scripts when called via "profile" so <script> will have to be a full 	pathname.	Make sure that the "/pipe/setterm" is empty before running setterm or 	it may hang if the pipe becomes full. If the termcap definition for your	terminal is complex it can result in a 'setenv TERMCAP' command that 	exceeds the MicroWare shell's maximum command size of 511 characters, so 	setterm will omit the command. setterm can be forced to exclude this	command by the -x option.Carsten Emde's shell "sh" and "ksh"-----------------------------------	The options "-si" should be used in this environment to force input to 	be read from the standard input (which is not redirected within scripts)	and to send output to the standard output for use by the `...` construct.	The commands form a single line consisting of a variable assignment for 	each environment variable being set followed by an "export" command to 	make the variables visible outside the script. Statements are separated 	by semi-colons.	Setterm will be usually be run from the login profile script 	("$HOME/.profile" for "sh" and	"/dd/sys/envi.ksh" for "ksh"). 	The generated commands are on one line to enable them to be read via 	the `...` variable assignment mechanism (or $(..) for "ksh") and 	executed via "eval". For example, ".profile" might contain:		PATH=.:/dd/CMDS:/dd/PD/CMDS:/dd/LOCAL/CMDS		chx /dd/local/cmds						# the path is now set up so 		export PATH								# setterm can always be found		changes=`unsetenv TERM; setterm -si`	# put commands in changes		eval $changes							# execute them	The "unsetenv TERM" command is needed with "sh" to cancel the default	value of "unknown" (set up by "sh" during the `...` operation). It is 	not needed by "ksh". Neither sh nor ksh have a fixed limit to the size	of a command and so setterm will always include the TERMCAP assignment	and export unless the -x option is present.Any other shell---------------	"/pipe/setterm" contains the following pair of commands on separate	lines:		echo Shell xxxxx not recognised by setterm		echo No environment variables set